  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light rain continues this afternoon into early \nMonday morning. Breezy, cloudy, and cooler Monday afternoon.\n\nA raw, cool, and rainy afternoon continues across southern New \nEngland, with rain persisting through much of the evening. Rainfall \nwill be predominantly stratiform with generally light to \noccasionally moderate rainfall rates. A rumble of thunder cannot be \nruled out near the south coast, though the risk is quite low. \nOverall, additional rainfall amounts of 0.25-0.75 inch are expected, \nwith some localized variability. Flooding is not a concern with this \nevent. Rain gradually shifts offshore overnight, coming to an end by \nor before daybreak Monday. It remains raw and wet overnight, with \nlows in the upper 40s to lower 50s and a breezy northerly wind.\n\nAs for Monday, showers linger just offshore along a frontal boundary \nwhile 1030 mb high pressure builds in from the northwest. This \nresults in a tightening pressure gradient and breezy north-northeast \nwinds throughout the day, especially near the coast where gusts top \nout around 20-25 mph. Clouds hang tough for much of the day, though \nthere may be a few glimmers of sunshine. It will remain cool, with \nhighs ranging through the 60s. Clouds remain Monday night as well, \nwith lows falling into the 40s across much of the region, while the \nurban centers remain in the lower 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure will yield mainly dry conditions \nand cool temperatures the first half of the week. Potential for \nan offshore coastal low late week. There is still uncertainty in\nthe track and strength at this time. \n\nThere is good agreement across model guidance with a fairly \nstrong surface high shifting over Quebec Tuesday into late-week.\nThis will put the region under a cooler than normal airmass \nwith consistent NE flow. This will yield cooler than normal \nhighs in the 60s through the week. There is a low probability \nthat a system just brushes southern New England Tuesday bringing\na chance for showers mainly confined to the immediate south \ncoast and Cape/Islands.\n\nDeterministic and ensemble guidance continues highlight potential \nfor a slow moving offshore coastal storm developing Wednesday into \nlate-week. There is a general consensus with an upper low and trough \nfirst tracking eastward across the upper mid-west, although cluster \nanalysis does highlight some variance in the amplitude of the trough \nand track of the  low. As this pushes to the east coast, a coastal \nlow develops in the Atlantic then tracking northward toward the \nnortheastern coastline. As with most coastal storms this far out, \nthere is still a notable amount of variance across guidance in how \nthis low tracks and its strength. This will directly feed into the \nuncertainty in elements such as winds, rain chances/amounts, and \ncoastal erosion/flooding concerns. \n\nWith a strong high to the north and an incoming upper low, the \npressure gradient will tighten Tuesday through the week. This will \nresult in strengthening winds/gusts across the coastal waters, \nespecially the southern waters in closer proximity to the stronger \ngradient. There is some question on how strong the gradient gets and \nwhere the strong part of the gradient positions and this will\nlikely depend on the track and strength of the low. All that \nsaid, ensemble guidance is signaling potential for gale force \ngusts for the southern waters. Strong gusts may develop as early\nas Tuesday night, but more likely Wednesday morning. Strong \nwinds are likely to stay as the coastal low tracks northward; \nhowever, the magnitude will depend more on the track and \nstrength of the low itself which remains less certain at this \ntime. For land, winds will be gusty as well although not as \nhigh in magnitude as the coastal waters(25-35 mph) Wed- late \nweek. Cape and Islands will likely be on the higher range of the\ngusts. If the low ends up tracking more west, periods of rain \nwill be possible sometime in the Thursday to Sat timeframe.",
